<section class="soc-sec soc-howto"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">How to read the ladders</h2><p>Each ladder runs from the top of its society to the bottom, by rank in law and honour rather than by wealth alone: a rich Roman freedman still stood below the freeborn. Every rank has a share of the whole population, children included: from the scholarship where scholars have counted or estimated one, and otherwise an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies. To compare societies, every rank is also placed in one of eight broad kinds: ruler, nobility and officials, religious and learned, soldiers, merchants and craftsmen, free commoners, serfs and dependants, outcast groups, enslaved.</p></section>
